# Notifications, acknowledgement and alerts

Name the message’s audience, delivery channel and current evidence. Sending a notification, displaying it, dismissing it and resolving the underlying condition are separate events. Do not infer that someone saw a message from a successful request or a disappearing badge.

## Scope and native ownership

Native persistent notifications can be created and dismissed in the frontend. Other integrations have their own delivery and action contracts. The civica sidebar badge presents native attention counts; it does not implement delivery, reading receipts or repair resolution. [Official Home Assistant source](https://www.home-assistant.io/integrations/persistent_notification/), reviewed 11 November 2026.

This page completes the design specification for **HA-GAP-028**. Its examples are synthetic documentation, not a claim that a new native feature or adapter has been installed. The application integration must meet the task-specific acceptance below.

## States and content decisions

| Source condition | Required presentation | Boundary |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Request sent / accepted | Show the outcome actually returned by the sending integration. | Do not label it Read or Delivered without that evidence. |
| Frontend message present | Keep a useful route to its details. | Appearance does not establish which person read it. |
| Acknowledged / dismissed | Name the operation and its scope. | Dismissed does not mean the underlying condition is resolved. |
| Resolved | Use the actual source condition or explicit workflow outcome. | A zero count alone is not proof of successful repair. |
| Repeated unchanged condition | Update the existing message where supported. | Do not announce every sensor refresh as a new emergency. |

## Integration contract

1. Choose the native delivery mechanism and account/device audience explicitly. A shared dashboard, phone push and persistent notification have different visibility and persistence.
2. Map only status fields the backend exposes. If no delivery/read receipt exists, state that limitation instead of deriving one from an action response.
3. Use a stable message identity when the integration supports updating an existing record. Keep dismissal, acknowledgement and condition state separate in the implementation.
4. Any action button must retain native permission checks and identify its consequence. Do not disguise a consequential command as an ordinary View details link.

## Accessible interaction

- Keep message detail reachable with a named link. Numeric badges supplement it and must not be the only explanation.
- Announce newly relevant outcomes once with appropriate live-region urgency; preserve a readable notification history where the native system provides it.
- Adapt previews to shared-screen choices without changing the underlying meaning. Do not expose private names, precise locations or security details by default in public examples.

## Failure and recovery

- If sending fails, explain which channel failed and whether other channels have independent confirmed results.
- If an acknowledgement request is unconfirmed, preserve that uncertainty and the original condition.
- For a recurring problem, avoid escalating severity solely because it has appeared often; use the source’s actual urgency definition.

